"""Question-independent writer and hierarchy manager contracts for event-only hierarchy (v3)."""

WRITER = '''You observe a causal video segment. Write a small set of atomic observations, NOT one caption covering the whole scene.
Separate different actions: e.g. skiing downhill, stopping, and greeting are three actions even if the same person performs them. Preserve repeated occurrences separately. One continuing action across several frames is one observation. Do not invent motion, intentions, identity, text, or causal explanations from static frames. State uncertainty explicitly. The frames may omit intervening actions.
Write observations only for observed actions or verifiable changes. A stable background, unchanged posture, or stationary object alone is not an event. If no action or change is supported, return {"observations":[]} rather than inventing an action_key. Retain relevant background attributes in the actors, objects and location fields of actual events; there is no separate persistent state collection. A single pose does not establish a movement or transition.
Return exactly one JSON object with an observations list (0 to 6 entries). Each entry must have exactly these keys:
{"summary":"short observed fact", "action_key":"short canonical verb phrase such as skiing downhill", "actors":["visible distinguishing attributes"], "objects":["visible objects"], "state_changes":["object: observed before -> observed after"], "location":"observed location or empty", "visible_text":["text legible in a frame"], "uncertainty":["what cannot be established"], "evidence_ids":["frame IDs supporting THIS observation"]}
Use supplied frame IDs only. Each observation needs at least one evidence frame. Each list at most 6 short entries, summary at most 35 words. Use empty lists when unsupported. Do not assign observation IDs or times; the program does that. No markdown, explanation or trailing notes.'''

MANAGER = '''Manage the memory of a video observed so far. There are three different concepts:
1. A segment is a low-level sampling interval. It may contain several different actions.
2. An atomic EVENT is ONE observed action or verifiable change involving particular participants. Static scene descriptions are not standalone events. A new action, state transition, distinct repetition, or uncertain identity normally starts a NEW event.
3. An EPISODE is a larger observed process that can contain MANY DISTINCT events. It is appropriate for skiing, stopping, then greeting to be three events under one outing episode. Putting events under one episode does NOT mean they are one continuous atomic event.

Allocate pending observations to events, and organize events into episodes only when justified. Do not force a chosen number of parents. Do not delete, fuse, reorder or hide observation instances. Empty pending lists need no new event. Return ONLY one JSON object:
{"events":[{"op":"new", "id":"E1", "observation_ids":["S0001:O01"], "summary":"short observed atomic event", "action_key":"canonical action", "entities":["participants"], "continuation_reason":"", "support_evidence_ids":["f0001"]}], "episodes":[{"id":"P1", "summary":"short up-to-date account of the observed process and its child actions", "event_ids":["E1"], "status":"open"}]}

For a NEW event, use an unused E ID, one atomic action, and an empty continuation_reason. Copy action_key exactly from its writer observation. Never replace distinct writer actions with a more general action; use separate E IDs. Its evidence must support the new observations. Allocate every pending observation exactly once in the response.
For CONTINUING an existing event, set op="continue", use its existing E ID and EXACT existing action_key, which must also match the incoming writer observation action_key, include only newly pending observation_ids, give a nonempty continuation_reason, and cite support_evidence_ids from BOTH the old event and the new observations. You must have those old and new frames in the supplied visual evidence. Same person, same scene, or adjacent time ALONE is insufficient. A different action or incompatible action_key needs a new E ID. If identity/continuity is unclear, prefer a new event and retain uncertainty.
Use only observed/current IDs. An observation may be allocated once. Existing observations and evidence remain immutable.

For episodes, list the FULL member event_ids when creating or updating. New episodes use unused P IDs. Existing events cannot move between parents or be removed from a parent. Events may remain standalone if grouping is uncertain. Every change to an event ALREADY inside an episode MUST include an update of that episode in this SAME response, with an up-to-date summary. When adding new events to a parent, likewise update its full event_ids and summary. Include all previously observed stages needed to characterize the process; do not preserve an obsolete first-segment summary. Status is open or closed. Do not describe unseen future goals.
Each summary at most 60 words. Each decision at most 8 event updates and 4 episode updates. No markdown, explanations, or notes outside JSON.'''

REPAIR = '''Your previous response violated the output contract or referenced invalid memory objects. Correct the JSON using ONLY the original supplied observations and evidence. Do not invent missing observations, IDs, entities, or future events. Return one complete JSON object only. The validation error describes an interface constraint, not a fact about the video.'''
